    "That collective bouyon has its own grammar, different from the live band bouyon of 1990s Dominica told on our <a href=\"/en/history.html\">history of bouyon</a> page. Here, tracks are short, credits are shared between collective voices, beatmakers and DJs, and the team signature, Team Bwe Tou Sa or T-BTS, works as a mark of manufacture. FLW is an exact product of that grammar: a short three letter name, always published with his team mention, collaboration tracks before the track under his own name, and an official video as the centrepiece. His documented path, from “RAKITA” to “BIG BAG”, follows the usual slope of the collective: enter through the team tracks, then sign your own.",
    "The genre itself keeps its roots written in plain sight: bouyon was born in Dominica, and its soca branch was started in Dominica in 2003 by Dada, according to the testimony of Smokiller published on this site. The French Caribbean collective wave, of which FLW is part, is one of the forms that Dominican sound takes in 2026 as it circulates between Guadeloupe, Martinique and the mainland diaspora. Our <a href=\"/en/playlist-bouyon.html\">bouyon playlist</a> and our <a href=\"/en/bouyon-gwada.html\">bouyon gwada</a> page follow that circulation track by track."
